# WC(1)

## NAME

wc - word count

## SYNOPSIS

| **wc** [ **-lwc** ] [ name ... ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Wc* counts lines, words and characters in the named files, or in the standard input if no name
appears. A word is a maximal string of characters delimited by spaces, tabs or newlines.

If the optional argument is present, just the specified counts (lines, words or characters) are
selected by the letters **l**, **w**, or **c**.

**Note:** the counts always come out in the order **l**, **w**, **c**, whatever order the
letters were given in, and a bare **-** selects none of them - *wc* then reads its input and
prints an empty line. Both are v7's.

## WORDS, CHARACTERS AND EIGHT BITS

**Note:** a **character** here is a **byte**. This machine's text is UTF-8, so a Cyrillic
letter is two bytes and counts as two.

**Note:** a byte above **0177** is a **word constituent,** not a delimiter; only space, tab and
newline end a word. v7's test was **\` ' < c && c < 0177** - printable ASCII and nothing else -
which on this machine would have made `привет мир` zero words rather than two, every
byte of it being 0320 or over. The test is **c > \` ' && c != 0177** here.

The DEL exclusion is v7's and is kept, as is what follows from it: a control character other
than tab and newline neither ends a word nor begins one, so a word with one embedded in it
still counts as one word.

## DIAGNOSTICS

A file that cannot be opened is reported on the diagnostic output and skipped; the remaining
arguments are still counted, and a **total** line still appears.

**Note:** *wc* then exits **1**. v7 exited 0 however many files it had failed to open, so no
shell script could tell a run that counted everything from one that counted half of it.

## SEE ALSO

sum(1)
